    "encrypted": "false",
    "long_instructions": "\r\n\r\nStudents will build on their understanding of arrays by creating a schema and using it to design an app that handles personal data. \r\n\r\n<div style=\"background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 95%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;\">\r\n<p style=\"font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;\">💡 <b>Teacher Tip:</b> This is a good moment to remind students that personal data (like allergies, medications, or health information) should be handled thoughtfully and stored only for learning purposes.</p>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n## Schema Design Activity\r\n\r\nStudents can choose to design a Food Allergy Tracker, a Medication Reminder, or create their own idea. In this professional learning session, we’ll focus on the **Food Allergy Tracker** so you can see how schema and parallel arrays work together in a full example.\r\n\r\n## Example Schema\r\n\r\nStudents will start by brainstorming on their own and then use AI to help refine or expand their schema. This example schema uses the fields of allergy name, severity, symptoms, and treatment to store data. \r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://images.code.org/8946e446da983c617f39ca66ee8f578d-image-1765557163145.png\" alt=\"schema for allergy app: allergen name, severity, symptoms, treatment\" width=\"450px\" style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; \">\r\n\r\n## Activity Tutorial\r\n\r\nThe video below walks through the steps of the allergy tracker activity. Watch the video to get a basic understanding of the activity, and then try it on your own. Feel free to rewatch the video as needed.  \r\n"
  },
